# API Security Conventions

## WebSocket Gateways

- CORS must use the env-driven allowlist, never `origin: '*'`
- Never combine `origin: '*'` with `credentials: true`
- All gateways must have `@SkipThrottle()` (global ThrottlerGuard is active)

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
@WebSocketGateway({ cors: { origin: '*' } })

// ✅ GOOD
@WebSocketGateway({
  cors: {
    origin: (process.env.CORS_ORIGINS || 'https://app.privateconnect.co,https://privateconnect.co')
      .split(',').map(s => s.trim()).filter(Boolean),
  },
})
```

## Authentication

- Authenticated routes must use `AuthGuard`, `CombinedAuthGuard`, or `ApiKeyGuard`
- Public routes should be explicitly intentional (health, status, temporary tunnels, ask)
- Admin routes must use `AdminGuard`

## Tokens and Secrets

- Bearer-style tokens must use at least `randomBytes(16)` (128 bits)
- Never return `err.message` to clients — use static error strings
- Never hardcode secrets, API keys, or environment-specific URLs — use `process.env`

## Input Validation

- Global `ValidationPipe` is active with `whitelist` and `forbidNonWhitelisted`
- New DTOs should use `class-validator` decorators
- Zod schemas in controllers are also acceptable

## Database

- Always use Prisma parameterized queries or tagged template literals for raw SQL
- WebSocket handlers don't run the HTTP `ContextInterceptor` — verify RLS context manually if querying tenant data
- Enforce body size limits on proxy and upload routes via `NETWORK_CONFIG.MAX_REQUEST_BODY_SIZE`

## Rate Limiting

- `ThrottlerGuard` is registered as a global `APP_GUARD`
- Use `@Throttle()` to override limits on specific routes
- Use `@SkipThrottle()` on health checks, WebSocket gateways, and agent-facing endpoints

## Error Responses

- Never leak stack traces, file paths, or DB errors to clients
- Use `HttpException` subclasses with user-safe messages

## Docs, Rules, and Committed Files

This is a public open-source repo. Never commit:

- Real API keys, tokens, passwords, or connection strings — use placeholders (`pc_xxx`, `gnt_...`, `YOUR_API_KEY`)
- Real workspace IDs, agent IDs, or user IDs — use RFC 4122 example UUIDs or obviously fake values
- Internal hostnames, non-RFC 1918 IPs, or private infrastructure URLs
- `.env` files, `credentials.json`, or any file containing live secrets
- Real rate limit bypass techniques, vulnerability details, or unpatched exploit info

When writing docs or Cursor rules:

- Env var names are fine (`DATABASE_URL`, `CORS_ORIGINS`) — values are not
- Public endpoints can be listed (they're in Swagger already) — auth bypass patterns cannot
- Architecture descriptions are fine — implementation-specific security weaknesses are not
- Use `process.env.VAR` in code examples, never inline secret values
